  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1253 PM MDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nNORMAL TEMPERATURES WITH A FEW ISOLATED SHOWERS TODAY:\n\nHigh temperatures will be near normal today despite warm \nsouthwesterly flow thanks to substantial afternoon cloud cover.\nThis cloud cover is associated with moisture from Tropical Storm \nOdalys and Hurricane Polo, which is just beginning to advect into \nour region. This increased moisture brings chances of a few isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms late this afternoon, mostly over the \nhigher terrain. Chances of precipitation increases for the central \nand northern Divide this evening. Overnight chances will increase in \nthe Four Corners region, gradually spreading northward across the \nCWA through the morning hours. \n\nWIDESPREAD PRECIPITATION MONDAY AND TUESDAY: \n\nMoisture advection from the tropical storm activity in the Pacific \nwill bring anomalous moisture to our region through at least \nmidweek, with precipitable water anomalies peaking around 250% of \nnormal on Monday. This moisture will be accompanied by synoptic \nforcing as a closed upper level low digs southward through \nCalifornia on Monday, and then across the Southwest on Tuesday and \nWednesday. On Monday energy downstream of the trough axis will move \nacross our area along with a jet streak aloft, providing lift to \nsupport widespread precipitation. This widespread precipitation will \ncontinue through most of Tuesday  until the trough axis moves across \nthe Divide. The potential for prolonged periods of rainfall, \nincluding in areas that have recently received substantial rain, \nraises flooding concerns particularly in southeast Utah and \nsouthwest Colorado where a Flood Watch is in effect from 6AM Monday \nthrough 6AM Wednesday.\nOn Wednesday the low and elevated mositure will move east of the \nRocky Mountains, bringing drier weather the the area. However, \nlingering moisture may be able to support some additional convection \nover the high terrain on Wednesday.",
